  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 505,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 544,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70601,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 544,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Paratestis or testis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:32:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 506,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 545,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70602,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 545,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The presenting manifestation is testicular enlargement due to a paratesticular mass or a lesion located deeper in the testicular parenchyma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:32:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 507,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 546,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70603,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 546,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are uncommon tumours that occur over a wide age range, including in children (range: 7&ndash;67&nbsp;years) {28579069}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:32:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 508,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 547,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70604,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 547,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The origin is speculative; the tumours are hypothesized to originate from M&uuml;llerian metaplasia of the tunica vaginalis or from M&uuml;llerian rests in the paratesticular soft tissue, appendix testis, and scrotum {15663695}. M&uuml;llerian rests, which also exist in the female genital tract, may provide the cells of origin for low-grade serous neoplasia in both sexes {25491441}; this may also explain the paucity or absence in the testis of high-grade serous carcinoma arising from tubal-type epithelium [[WHO Classification of Tumours Editorial Board. Female genital tumours. Lyon (France): International Agency for Research on Cancer; 2020. (WHO classification of tumours series, 5th ed.; vol. 4). https://publications.iarc.fr/592.]].</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:32:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 509,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 548,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70605,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 548,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:32:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70606,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Serous cystadenocarcinomas are solid or cystic with excrescences. The colour is variable (greyish white, yellow, or tan) {7503358; 32619438}. Tumour diameter ranges from 15 to 115&nbsp;mm.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:32:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70607,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Serous cystadenocarcinoma shows infiltrating and well-formed papillary, tubular, or cribriform structures lined by a serous-type epithelium. The tumour cells lie within a characteristic desmoplastic stroma with associated psammoma bodies. Unlike in the ovary, where high-grade tumours represent the most common serous types, in the testis the majority are consistent with low-grade serous carcinoma {25491441} and the surrounding areas may contain remnants of a serous borderline tumour {32619438; 15842644; 20133965}. Low-grade serous carcinoma of the testis shows occasional prominent nucleoli {28579069} and low-grade morphology {28543997; 33102004}. Uncommon cases with high-grade atypia have been also reported {25308020; 28764180}.</p>\r\n<p>Immunohistochemistry is positive for PAX8, AE1/AE3, CK8/18, CK7, ER, EMA, WT1, BerEP4, CA125, and CK5/6. Immunohistochemistry is negative for CK20, inhibin, calretinin, PLAP, CD30, OCT4, and AFP {28579069; 28543997; 33102004}.</p>\r\n<p>Mesothelioma, adenocarcinoma of the rete testis, and adenocarcinoma of the epididymis should be considered in the differential diagnosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:32:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 511,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 550,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70608,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 550,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:32:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
